WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.802 --> 00:00:03.670
There was some degree
of runaway expectations.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.140
I think there was also some degree

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.170
that they weren't extremely forthcoming

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.780
with the state of the game.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.960
The Cyberpunk 2077 story is very much kind

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.120
of a story of everything they can

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.423
and will go wrong in game development.

00:00:20.259 --> 00:00:22.120
What is this bug?

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.510
Jack, his AI flipped out.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.300
It wasn't there before.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.150
Where's my gun?

00:00:27.362 --> 00:00:29.470
I can't shoot.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.310
The release of Cyberpunk 2077 in December

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.410
of 2020 will go down as one of the most

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.020
disastrous game launches in history,

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.410
and it instantly rewrote the narrative

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.560
of it's celebrated
developer, CD Projekt Red.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.540
Larger studios with bigger
budgets weren't able

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.130
to create the same quality
of open world game.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.590
The inside story of the making

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:52.720
of Cyberpunk depicts a process marred

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.990
by unchecked ambition,
unrealistic timelines,

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.450
and a focus on marketing at
the expense of development.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.270
When CD Projekt stock was at its high

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.640
it was the largest stock
in the Polish stock market.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.610
Cyberpunk was supposed to
be the biggest game of 2020.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.170
This was a game that was
starring Keanu Reeves.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.280
This is a game that was

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.710
from the makers of The Witcher Three.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.240
This was supposed to be the big
temple release of last year,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.790
and for it to become such a
disaster for so many people,

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:25.333
I think will have pretty
far reaching consequences.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.120
CD Projekt's origins are somewhat unique

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.400
in the games industry.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.160
In the West and Japan, the
late eighties and nineties

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.850
were dominated by games for
the home console market,

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.840
and characters like Mario
and Sonic the Hedgehog

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.830
were becoming household names.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.890
The same was not true in a post Soviet

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.270
and economically depressed Poland.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.377
Most gaming was done on PC's
and most games were copied

00:01:52.377 --> 00:01:55.440
and sold in small markets
around the country.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.680
You could go to the market down the street

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.270
and buy and sell CD's,

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.450
and these two guys of and Marcin Iwiński

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.000
and Michal Kiciński
decided they were going

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.520
to start selling CD's and
they wound up striking a deal

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.350
with the people who made a
game called Baldur's Gate,

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.930
which was kind of like the
Seminole role-playing game

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:15.682
in the late nineties.

00:02:15.682 --> 00:02:16.910
It was never a business.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.410
It was kind of lifestyle.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:22.190
So we're actually pretty
bad with counting money.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.180
So you can kind of think
of it almost like Nike,

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.710
you know, where Phil
Knight was selling shoes

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.240
out of the trunk of his car, you know.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.030
You can kind of think
about it the same way.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.620
It was, you know, just
selling physical goods

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.780
and then evolving that into
a much broader enterprise.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.920
CD Projekt, named for
selling CD's, would move

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.920
on from just reselling
games to localizing Western

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.530
and Japanese made games
into the Polish language,

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.280
but it wasn't until 2002
that the company would make

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.103
its own game, The Witcher.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.580
The Witcher is a huge, huge series,

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.580
huge fantasy series in Poland,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.860
and so it was a pretty
big deal that these guys

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.070
got the raise to make the video games.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.650
The Witcher book series as
a source of national pride

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.780
in Poland and as CD Projekt
released The Witcher Two,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.060
the video game series
would become an important

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.130
economic symbol for Poland, as well.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.440
CD Projekt Red had become this behemoth

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.330
of an institution in Poland
and they were really big

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.370
in the Polish technology scene because

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.190
they were getting more worldwide attention

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:24.420
than most Polish companies
do, and making a lot of money,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.070
and in 2011, when Barack Obama came

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:29.440
to visit Poland, the prime
minister actually gave

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.662
him a copy of The Witcher Two.

00:03:31.662 --> 00:03:33.912
I confess I'm not very
good at video games,

00:03:34.828 --> 00:03:39.828
but I've been told that
it is a great example

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:46.560
of Poland's place in
the new global economy.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.370
But it would be the launch
of The Witcher Three in 2015

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.357
that would elevate CD Projekt
Red from representing the best

00:03:51.357 --> 00:03:55.503
of Poland to representing the
best of the games industry.

00:04:07.429 --> 00:04:08.930
I mean really, the thing people look

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.700
for first and foremost, I think, is

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.840
that the game is just
good, and Witcher Three

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.793
was just a phenomenal game.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.690
There's a level of just
storytelling, an emphasis

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:24.180
on making every side quest
count, on top of the atmosphere,

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.600
the dialogue, the character interactions,

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.680
the different ways stories can end.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:29.980
They nail that.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.790
Other open-world games
felt empty and forced,

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.490
and the developers of them gave excuses

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:41.210
as to why there couldn't be
more rich story or interactions.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.700
And The Witcher Three blew
them out of the water.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.930
CD Projekt Red was beloved.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.860
They were seen as a
studio that was willing

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.260
to make what the player wanted
and to truly put in the love

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.190
and effort required to
make a high quality game

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:59.032
that really connects with
the people playing with it.

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:02.310
And the winner for Game of the Year 2015

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.270
is The Witcher Three.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.142
The Witcher Three would go on to win

00:05:08.142 --> 00:05:09.610
over 250 Game of the Year awards,

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.740
but along with being a critical success

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:14.623
for CD Projekt, it was
also a financial success.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.240
Estimates put the sales of Witcher Three

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.280
at around 28,000,000 copies in 2019,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.660
a huge step-up considering by 2014

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:25.140
The Witcher and The Witcher
Two had only combined

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.880
to sell 8,000,000 copies.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.260
And with two large expansions
for The Witcher Three,

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:31.240
CD Projekt also helps separate themselves

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.483
from other AAA publishers at the time.

00:05:34.439 --> 00:05:36.760
And the Game Award goes to, oh man,

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.780
I have to pay a
microtransaction to unlock?

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.743
That's so stupid that this has to,

00:05:43.948 --> 00:05:46.520
hold on, I got this guys, here we go.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.700
Other games, and the most
notorious, you know, one

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.160
to do this was EA's Star
Wars Battlefront Two.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.213
They got really aggressive
on microtransactions

00:05:56.213 --> 00:05:59.540
in a game that already costs at least $60.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:00.530
You could have paid more

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.370
if you bought the premium editions,

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.090
but not only was it that they
were being very aggressive

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:06.950
and trying to get you to spend money,

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.600
but you could actually get
items that helped you win.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:14.060
Well, gamers kind of draw the line

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.950
in the sand there and
they don't like that.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.440
CD Projekt had done this phenomenal job

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.170
of marketing themselves as
a gamer friendly company.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:24.210
We've said this a million
times and I'll say it again.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.560
We're gamers, first of all,

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.540
and we all like to be treated fairly,

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.720
so it only makes sense that as developers

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.590
we apply the same principle to everything

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.590
we do here at CD Projekt Red,
so giving everyone a bunch

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.273
of free DLC was an absolutely no brainer.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.768
They said, "We're not gonna sell you DLC,

00:06:40.768 --> 00:06:43.490
that's like horse armor
packs than other nonsense.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.780
We're gonna just give you free stuff

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.220
and then we're gonna sell big expansions

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:49.950
as part of The Witcher Three."

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.520
And not only were they really good,

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.770
they were offered at a fair price.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:56.643
And this is a company
that was seen as just

00:06:56.643 --> 00:06:59.220
like a company that
really cared about quality

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.160
and cared about its customers,
and would not do anything

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:06.290
to kind of screw fans over the way

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.090
that fans see that EA and Activision

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.540
and all the other big publishers do.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:13.970
With a critically and
financially successful game,

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.440
as well as the Goodwill
from the gaming audience,

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:17.500
all the focus would shift

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:21.173
to CD Projekt's next
release, Cyberpunk 2077.

00:07:28.699 --> 00:07:29.659
It's actually funny.

00:07:29.659 --> 00:07:30.580
They first announced it
through like a press release,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.280
and then they showed off
a trailer at the beginning

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:36.248
of 2013 that was like the
CGI trailer of like a woman

00:07:36.248 --> 00:07:38.480
and it was all sorts of
cool cyberpunk stuff,

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.380
flying cars lots of stuff,
but it wasn't really

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:45.508
until after The Witcher Three
that Cyberpunk began really

00:07:45.508 --> 00:07:48.280
like building up this
feverish level of hype.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:49.960
CD Projekt Red, though, wouldn't put

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.670
out another trailer for Cyberpunk

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.700
until 2018 at the E3 Trade Show.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.680
And they showed off a, it was

00:07:57.744 --> 00:07:59.953
like a two minute trailer of
Cyberpunk, looked pretty rad.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:10.360
And then they showed behind the scenes

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:13.210
for E3 attendees, for
press, and anyone else

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:15.760
who could score an appointment in there.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.596
They showed this forty-five minute demo

00:08:18.596 --> 00:08:22.530
and it blew people away.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.670
Welcome to the gameplay demo walkthrough

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:28.990
of CD Projekt Red's upcoming
title, Cyberpunk 2077.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:30.710
The gameplay you're about to see is

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:32.910
from a work in progress
version of the game.

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:35.733
Everything you see is
potentially subject to change.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:38.857
Holy .

00:08:41.108 --> 00:08:43.090
This, if they can live up to what

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:46.933
they showed me, it's game over.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:51.020
It all looked incredibly impressive.

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:54.480
They had enough action,
enough narrative beats

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.710
with just the right amount of tension.

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.640
They touted how each of these NPC's,

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.450
there are thousands of them, each one

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.650
with their own daily
routines and everything.

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:06.320
We've greatly enhanced our
crowd and community system

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.850
to create the most believable city

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:11.610
in any open world game to date.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.671
So, yeah, so people were
certainly blown away

00:09:14.671 --> 00:09:15.860
by this demo.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:18.620
It won E3 Awards and just people left

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:21.250
the theater just in awe.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:22.470
What they didn't know, of course, is

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:25.010
that the demo was completely fake.

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:26.810
Fake demos are common at E3.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:28.283
Studios put together builds of games

00:09:28.283 --> 00:09:30.240
that show off what they intend the game

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.863
to be like, rather than the
actual state of the game.

00:09:32.863 --> 00:09:34.641
What was unique about this demo was

00:09:34.641 --> 00:09:37.440
that it was impressively long.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.700
It kind of plays into
this idea that CD Projekt

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:41.650
is very much a marketing driven company,

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:44.140
and so demos were really,
really important to them.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.170
So it was really important
that they make this demo

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.500
blow people away, which it did,

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.850
but that might've come at
the cost of like some time

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.670
they could have been spending on the game.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:55.100
A year later at E3 2019,

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.199
fans would get another big surprise.

00:09:59.846 --> 00:10:02.701
Whoa, no way!

00:10:02.701 --> 00:10:07.275
Oh, what!

00:10:07.275 --> 00:10:12.275
Please welcome Keanu Reeves.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.640
Of being there, of walking the streets

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:19.253
of the future is really
going to be breathtaking.

00:10:20.289 --> 00:10:22.735
You're breathtaking!

00:10:22.735 --> 00:10:26.476
You're breathtaking.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:34.130
The Cyberpunk trailers
showed gorgeous cities,

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:38.060
beautiful lighting, really
cutting edge technology

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.420
when it comes to the
rendering of the game itself.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:43.490
People were very excited to be immersed

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.090
in this gorgeous cyberpunk world.

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:50.440
They saw over 8,000,000
pre-orders, which is a ridiculously

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:54.820
large amount for any title
to sell, let alone pre-sell.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.330
And you saw the stock price start

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.630
to go up and up and up and the right,

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:00.670
you know, pretty much in a straight line.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:02.810
If you just look at like
the Polish stock market,

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:05.870
like the WIG Index, when CD
Projekt stock was at its high,

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:09.450
it was the largest stock in the index.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:10.823
And that was around the
time that they announced

00:11:10.823 --> 00:11:14.230
that the game would be
coming in April of 2020.

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.710
Behind the scenes people
were pretty shocked

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:17.820
that they were saying April.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:20.010
I don't think a single a person who worked

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.820
on that game actually
thought they had a chance

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:25.503
of coming out in April of 2020.

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:32.740
So in April of 2019, I
published an article for Kotaku

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.450
about the making of a game
called Anthem, which is a game

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.210
from BioWare that was
disastrous in many ways

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:41.040
and the article kind of
ran through why that was.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:42.400
And then afterwards, I started hearing

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.970
from other developers
who had stories to share

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:47.247
that they said sounded a
whole lot like Anthem's,

00:11:47.247 --> 00:11:50.870
"The deadline is unrealistic,
the direction keeps changing,

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:53.267
things are floundering,
we're going through

00:11:53.267 --> 00:11:55.110
a lot of problems, this sounds exactly

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:57.220
like what you wrote about Anthem."

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.150
Around that same time,
perhaps coincidentally,

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:03.300
I heard from the CEO of
CD Projekt Red who wanted

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:06.383
to reach out to me specifically
to talk about Crunch.

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:11.410
CD Projekt Red had a
reputation for Crunch,

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.670
especially during the
development of The Witcher Three.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.030
Their management wanted
them to have a reputation

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:19.490
for treating workers
well and not forcing them

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:21.690
to go through Crunch, so Marcin Iwiński,

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.690
who's the co-founder and
co CEO of CD Projekt Red,

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:27.780
came to me and said, "Hey, I
want to make a vow right now

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.170
that we're not gonna force anybody

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:30.450
to Crunch, that people will not have

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:33.637
to work mandatory
overtime on this project."

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:37.460
Over the course of reporting

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:39.100
Bloomberg has conducted
interviews with more

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.800
than twenty current and
former CD Projekt staff,

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:43.630
most of whom requested anonymity,

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:45.680
so as not to risk their careers.

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:47.880
CD Projekt declined to
comment on the process

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:49.730
or provide interviews for this story.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:52.470
The company would end up delaying

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.740
the game three different
times, first pushing

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:57.830
from an April 2020 release to September,

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:01.300
then November, and once
again, to December 2020.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.500
In order to meet the deadline,
the studio would scale

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.700
down the size of Cyberpunk's main city,

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:07.600
as well as canceling some features.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.050
And while those measures
helped, developers still

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:13.400
said they were under significant
crunch, including one

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:15.054
former audio programmer
who claimed to work up

00:13:15.054 --> 00:13:18.670
to thirteen hours a day, five
days a week, and stating he

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:21.970
had seen friends with lost
their families over this.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:25.980
This was not a big secret
in the office that the game

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:29.690
was not going to be
ready, but a similar sort

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:31.680
of thing happened with The
Witcher Three and a similar sort

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:34.732
of thing happens with a lot
of games, where they don't

00:13:34.732 --> 00:13:36.170
seem ready until suddenly
everything just clicks.

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:38.010
Some of the best games ever made,

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.130
like were created through this process.

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:41.720
I don't know that it's the most efficient

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:43.710
or the wisest way to make video games,

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:45.540
but it's what a lot of people do.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:49.030
I think that management
there has genuinely tried to

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:50.980
change it, or at least said
they wanted to change it,

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:55.520
but it has proven very
difficult to change that

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.640
when you make games in
this one specific way.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.550
Game studios, some of them see Crunch

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:03.440
as part of their core culture.

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.910
That all of their games that they've made

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:07.860
under Crunch have been successful,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.730
so it must be a core
element to the magic recipe

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:15.840
for creating good games, and
that's really not the case.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.880
We've run studies and seen that

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:22.610
at eight weeks of
working sixty hours plus,

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:26.620
the output of a professional is the same

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:28.530
as if they were working
forty hours a week,

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:33.530
so chronic overwork degrades
how effective someone is

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:36.170
to the point where then
it becomes negative,

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:38.340
and overworking them is just getting

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:40.830
you less and less productivity.

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:44.470
And so these guys, the
people at CD Projekt, having

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:47.210
made The Witcher Three, were
kind of riding on that high.

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:49.310
You could call it arrogance
or confidence or whatever

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:51.550
you want to call it, of
having made The Witcher Three,

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:53.950
and I think that like
having a certain level

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.572
of confidence can certainly
be a good thing, but

00:14:56.572 --> 00:14:59.411
in this case it could also
be dangerous because it kind

00:14:59.411 --> 00:15:01.710
of blinded them to the reality, which was

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.480
that this game wasn't going to coalesce.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.430
Cyberpunk was an ambitious project

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:09.510
by any standard, but for CD Projekt Red,

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:12.110
it was also a very large
departure from The Witcher.

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:16.630
Cyberpunk was a sci-fi world, rather

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:18.250
than a medieval fantasy.

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:20.230
Instead of a third
person game that revolved

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:22.530
around combat with swords and shields,

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:25.070
Cyberpunk was a first person shooter.

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:27.540
Making Cyberpunk would
require CD Projekt to invest

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:31.010
in new technology,
including its game engine.

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:36.010
Games have to have a lot
of logic behind them.

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:39.310
When someone talks
about developing a game,

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:42.400
particularly in their own
engine, they're not just talking

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.530
about creating art assets and setting

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.130
up quests, they're talking about building

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:51.460
the rendering systems,
figuring out the shaders

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:54.090
and graphic systems and physics systems.

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:56.910
It's a lot of low level work,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:16:00.950
and a lot of that low level
work prevents some testing

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:03.030
and progressing of the game forward.

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:06.390
Which is sort of like if
you were driving a train

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.020
while someone else was in front of you

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:10.670
like laying down tracks, as you went.

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:13.260
You're like trying to make
a movie while someone else

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.940
is building the camera at the same time.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.970
It's very, very difficult and
makes things go super slowly

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:21.480
and makes things go very inefficiently

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:22.880
and just hampers everything.

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:26.130
And that's actually why
game development studios

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:28.150
prefer open offices.

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:31.680
It's so that the team can see
what everyone else is working

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:34.730
on and have a passive sense of the state

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:36.780
of the game and how it's going.

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.740
But in the final months of development,

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.100
developers in Cyberpunk were mostly doing

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:42.790
this development from their homes.

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:45.300
Across Poland, all
malls, restaurants, clubs

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.580
and bars, closing down with a ban

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.580
on gatherings of more than fifty people.

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:56.070
I think because of COVID,
there was a lot of issues,

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:58.570
not just in terms of
development and the timing,

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:00.835
but also the communication side of it.

00:17:00.835 --> 00:17:04.730
And Quality Assurance is a
huge part of game development

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:06.480
that a lot of people don't talk about.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:10.220
QA testers help run the
game through its paces

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:13.000
to figure out any issues it may have.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:14.540
Then they write report tickets

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:16.910
and send it back to developers to fix.

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:19.630
It's easy to underestimate the amount

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:23.310
of time it'll take to fix
a few bugs that you find.

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:25.670
There's a little song that I appreciate.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:27.570
There's ninety-nine
little bugs on the wall,

00:17:27.570 --> 00:17:29.850
ninety-nine little bugs, take one down,

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:33.540
patch it around, 117 bugs in the code.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:35.670
Making that even more
difficult was just how

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:39.015
many different platforms
the game was coming out for.

00:17:39.015 --> 00:17:41.640
Due to the delays the game
was simultaneously coming out

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.490
for PCs and Google's new
streaming service, Stadia,

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:47.543
as well as the console
systems that would now include

00:17:47.543 --> 00:17:49.790
the next generation PS5 and Xbox X,

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:51.190
as well as the previous generation

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:54.230
of consoles, the PS4 and Xbox One.

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:56.420
And while the hardware and
the systems were in some cases

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:58.915
seven years old, they
represented a large market

00:17:58.915 --> 00:18:02.440
of console gamers,
particularly in the West.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:04.820
That is really, really
complicated, and porting

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:07.000
and dealing with like optimization

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:08.965
across the platforms can be
really, really challenging.

00:18:08.965 --> 00:18:13.430
And when you are a company
as CD Projekt Red is,

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:18.430
that primarily develops on PCs and is used

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:23.330
to making games for PC's,
sometimes not everybody

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:27.370
in the studio will be like
even bothering playing

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:30.290
the console versions, as
is what happened here.

00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:32.695
And what we saw with
Cyberpunk was a case where

00:18:32.695 --> 00:18:36.279
the console versions,
the console versions,

00:18:36.279 --> 00:18:38.860
were just a complete mess,
and when management announced

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:40.916
in October that the game had gone gold,

00:18:40.916 --> 00:18:42.690
meaning it was ready to
be pressed into discs,

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:45.110
there were still major
bugs being discovered.

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:47.810
Exhausted programmers would
scramble to fix as many

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:50.880
of these problems as they
could via a day one patch,

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:53.930
and CD Projekt did their best
to control which versions

00:18:53.930 --> 00:18:56.430
of the game would be seen
before the release date.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:18:59.720
So with the video game review process,

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:03.913
you are sent, first, some guidelines.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.430
Here's what to reveal and what not

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:10.170
to reveal when the game launches,

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:13.030
what spoilers are
important and what aren't,

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:15.300
and they give you these guidelines

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:18.170
alongside an NDA or some kind of embargo.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:20.190
When I read through the
embargo and the restrictions

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:22.950
I realized just how restrictive it was.

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:24.820
So reviews came out a few days

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:28.094
before the game came out
and they seemed pretty good,

00:19:28.094 --> 00:19:31.417
but reviewers all caution that they said,

00:19:31.417 --> 00:19:34.690
"Hey CD Projekt only let
us have the PC version.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:35.860
They didn't let us have access

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:39.470
to the PS4 and Xbox One
versions of the game."

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:42.307
The thinking at CD Projekt was maybe,

00:19:42.307 --> 00:19:45.040
"Okay, we have a patch
plan that maybe reviewers

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:46.910
didn't have access to,
like a day one patch,

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:48.590
so we don't want them to show footage

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.470
of the game before it's ready to go."

00:19:50.470 --> 00:19:52.385
And maybe they thought,
"Hey, the console versions,

00:19:52.385 --> 00:19:55.820
they're gonna get a patch like
the day before it comes out,

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:57.572
so we don't want to talk about that.

00:19:57.572 --> 00:19:59.300
We don't want to show
those versions just yet."

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:01.420
But no matter what the rationale was

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:03.970
behind CD Projekt doing this, ultimately

00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:06.990
it turned into the true scope

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.023
of the games problems being hidden.

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:14.440
On December 10th of 2020
the game was released,

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:17.236
and it didn't take long for
the problems become public.

00:20:17.236 --> 00:20:19.523
Oh, now it works.

00:20:20.876 --> 00:20:22.380
He T posed.

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.620
Stop, T...

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:32.880
Yeah, I mean obviously the
T posing stuff can be kind

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:34.860
of funny, but when it keeps happening

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:38.780
it really starts to break your immersion.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:42.923
My car would spawn where
another car was already at,

00:20:44.069 --> 00:20:45.072
and so they would like collide

00:20:45.072 --> 00:20:46.829
and then both cars would
explode or something.

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:51.350
There are a lot of like people's quests

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:53.930
ending unexpectedly,
or like AI's appearing.

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:57.620
One common one was that
someone who was supposed

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:01.010
to be just like with you on
one mission, like a buddy

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:02.980
with you on one mission
would just follow you

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:05.730
for the entire game and just inexplicably,

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:07.890
they would just be around with you.

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:10.000
One hilarious bug that I discovered when

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:13.730
I first loaded up the game
on launch day was there

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:17.960
were tiny trees covering every
single thing in the game.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:20.067
I was playing through the prologue

00:21:20.067 --> 00:21:21.160
and there were just
little trees everywhere.

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:25.500
I did try Cyberpunk 2077
on PlayStation Four,

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:28.689
and for me it's as good as unplayable

00:21:28.689 --> 00:21:30.653
with that level of performance.

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:37.940
There are segments where
it runs at like twenty-five

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:40.940
to thirty frames, but
then there are segments

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:42.540
where it's clear the
frame rates are at like,

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.960
your fifteens and twenties, and aiming

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.840
with an analog stake while
frame rates are rising

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:50.750
and dipping, it's simply impossible.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:53.300
The experience in those last
gen consoles will be so bad

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:55.570
that Sony would take
the unprecedented action

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:58.050
of removing the game
from its digital stores.

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.750
I mean, the stock started
selling off little by little

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:04.770
and then a lot by a lot, you
know, it kind of cascaded

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:08.500
to a point, and then once we saw Sony turn

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:11.700
off the ability to buy the
game and offer refunds,

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:15.983
that was kind of the drop
that the bottom fell out.

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:22.407
Yeah, so about a few weeks
after the game came out

00:22:22.407 --> 00:22:27.407
the CD Projekt's Co-founder
and Co-CEO Marcin Iwiński came

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:30.620
on a video and he spoke
for about five minutes

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:33.140
and tried to address
point by point, like some

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:35.670
of the things that people
have been talking about.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:38.930
Please don't fault any of
our teams for what happened.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:42.210
They all are incredibly
talented and hardworking.

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.870
Myself and the board are
the final decision makers

00:22:44.870 --> 00:22:47.140
and it was our call to release the game.

00:22:47.140 --> 00:22:48.780
It was just kind of
justifying their actions

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:50.675
and apologizing, and vowing

00:22:50.675 --> 00:22:53.720
that they would fix this
game, and they wanted

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:57.653
to regain people's trust,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:04.890
CD Projekt Red would
release three hot fixes

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:07.890
in December and two more
patches in the coming months.

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:10.690
In their apology video CD
Projekt Red would also release

00:23:10.690 --> 00:23:12.350
a roadmap for their future plans,

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:15.137
for both their DLC content
and future patches,

00:23:15.137 --> 00:23:17.130
although exactly when these things

00:23:17.130 --> 00:23:18.920
would come was a little vague.

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:20.270
It's to be seen what they do.

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:23.070
I mean, I think to save
face, they have to do what

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:25.684
they can to fix Cyberpunk,
but how much resources they

00:23:25.684 --> 00:23:30.684
dedicate to premium DLC
expansions to the online mode

00:23:31.249 --> 00:23:32.770
is still kind of up in the air.

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:35.250
We have to see what they say about that.

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:40.250
Or do you kind of just do what
you can, stop the bleeding

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.710
and just move on what you're for?

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:45.421
You know, at what point
do you have to just say,

00:23:45.421 --> 00:23:48.304
"We're gonna nip this
in the bud and move on?"

00:23:48.304 --> 00:23:50.780
It's gonna be a lot of focus for a lot

00:23:50.780 --> 00:23:52.580
of investors in the next few months.

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:57.700
There's a lot of precedent
in the video game industry

00:23:57.700 --> 00:23:59.587
for redemption stories,
for video games coming out

00:23:59.587 --> 00:24:04.250
in a pretty bad shape
and then getting fixed,

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:07.490
patched, updated, added to over time.

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:09.570
We've seen it with No Man's Sky,

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:11.040
we've seen it with Destiny, we've seen it

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.122
with Final Fantasy 14, a
lot of these online games.

00:24:13.122 --> 00:24:17.020
One thing that they could do is, say,

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:18.700
anyone who bought the game will get

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:21.580
like the DLC for free
as an apology from us.

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:22.870
There are a lot of things they could do,

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:24.770
I think, to win back the fans.

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:27.300
I think it's apparent
looking at the release

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:30.620
of Cyberpunk 2077 and the reception

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:34.390
of it that gamers overall
are much more tolerant

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:36.670
about pushed back release deadlines,

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:40.320
and understand that a game
that is rushed or built

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:43.250
under Crunch is less likely to come

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:46.150
out in the state that
they would like it to.

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:48.300
I make videos about these companies

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:50.620
and it's not because I want them to fail.

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.090
It's because I want them to get better.

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:55.070
I hope CD Projekt looks at
everything that's happening

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:56.630
and just actually makes some changes.

00:24:56.630 --> 00:24:59.370
I'm going to quote Shigeru
Miyamoto from Nintendo.

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:01.797
He's like the father of Super Mario,

00:25:01.797 --> 00:25:04.270
"A delayed game is eventually good.

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:06.620
A rushed game is never good."

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:09.840
And this is a company that
was not lacking for money.

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:12.270
They had plenty of cash,
and so the decision

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:16.343
to not do so will go down in
history as a very poor one.

